  [NEW · NOT_FOUND] absolute recurrence risk in that group is lower, so the same hazard ratio delivers a smaller absolute benefit
     draft credits : Morning Glory Sciences
     note          : No entity called 'Morning Glory Sciences' can be found in any source reached — no website, press release, publication, or commentary attributable to an organisation of that name exists in search results. The underlying epidemiological logic of the claim (lower absolute recurrence risk in stage IIB/IIC patients means a given hazard ratio yields smaller absolute benefit) is directionally contradicted by published trial data: stage IIC patients have a worse baseline prognosis (~50% 5-year recurrence risk) than stage IIIA/B, and peer-reviewed analyses of KEYNOTE-716 show that the absolute RFS benefit is actually higher in stage IIC (13.4%) than in stage IIB (13.2%), because the higher baseline risk amplifies the absolute gain from the same relative hazard ratio — the opposite of what the claim asserts. The attributed source 'Morning Glory Sciences' could not be located, and the factual premise of the claim is not supported by primary trial data.

