  [NEW · SERIOUS · FACT] The draft's own sources section (citing the JCO Oncology Advances trial design abstract, NCT05933577) lists overall survival as a secondary endpoint, not a key secondary endpoint. The Merck/Moderna press release and the ASCO Post both describe DMFS as 'a key secondary endpoint' and list OS simply as a secondary endpoint. Calling OS a 'key secondary endpoint' in this sentence overstates its pre-specified hierarchical status relative to DMFS, which was the designated key secondary.
     quote         : “the study will continue in order to evaluate other key secondary endpoints, including overall survival”
     evidence says : Overall survival is listed as a secondary endpoint in the trial design; DMFS was the designated key secondary endpoint that was tested and met at the interim analysis. OS is a secondary endpoint that will be evaluated at a future readout.
     fix           : Replace 'other key secondary endpoints, including overall survival' with 'other secondary endpoints, including overall survival'.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The relationship 'two-sided p ≈ 2 × one-sided p' holds only when the one-sided test is in the direction of the observed effect. The draft states this as a general rule without that qualification, which could mislead a reader into thinking the doubling is always exact or always applies. This is a minor calibration issue, not a serious error, but the omission of the directionality condition leaves the explanation incomplete.
     quote         : “A one-sided test asks only 'is it better?' A two-sided test asks 'is it different, in either direction?' For a symmetric test the two-sided p-value is about double the one-sided one, so the same data gives 0.0266 one way and 0.053 the other — identical evidence, measured against a different question.”
     evidence says : The two-sided p-value is approximately double the one-sided p-value when the one-sided test is in the direction of the observed effect (i.e., when the point estimate favours the treatment). The draft's arithmetic is correct for this case but the condition should be stated.
     fix           : Add after 'about double the one-sided one': '(when the one-sided test is in the direction of the observed effect, as it is here)'.

  [OVERLAP · SERIOUS · CONTRADICTION] The draft has already explained that the trial's prespecified analysis used a one-sided alpha of 0.10, not a two-sided alpha of 0.05. A two-sided 95% CI corresponds to a two-sided alpha of 0.05. The prespecified one-sided alpha of 0.10 corresponds to a two-sided 90% CI (or equivalently, a one-sided 95% CI). Saying the 95% CI 'is exactly what a two-sided 95% interval keys on' is correct as a description of what the Lancet paper reported, but the sentence implies this interval is the relevant inferential benchmark — when the trial's own registered threshold would correspond to a 90% two-sided CI. The draft's own p-value section explains the trial used a one-sided alpha of 0.10, making this sentence internally inconsistent with that explanation.
     already decided about this sentence, on other grounds: The gate is right: the clause asserted what a one-sided 90% interval would show without the arithmetic or a source. It has been removed from the page 
     If this is that same finding, it is settled. If it is a different
     fault in the same sentence, it is new and needs its own decision.
     quote         : “The confidence interval is the tell: 0.309–1.017 crosses 1.0, which is exactly what a two-sided 95% interval keys on.”
     evidence says : The 95% CI crossing 1.0 is consistent with a two-sided p > 0.05, which is how the Lancet paper presents it. But the trial's prespecified threshold was a one-sided alpha of 0.10, which corresponds to a two-sided 80% CI (or one-sided 90% CI) — not a two-sided 95% CI. The relevant inferential benchmark for this trial is not the 95% CI crossing 1.0.
     fix           : Replace 'The confidence interval is the tell: 0.309–1.017 crosses 1.0, which is exactly what a two-sided 95% interval keys on' with 'The 95% CI reported in the Lancet paper — 0.309–1.017 — crosses 1.0, consistent with the two-sided p of 0.053 the paper reports. But the trial's own prespecified threshold was a one-sided alpha of 0.10, against which 0.0266 was a clear pass; the 95% CI crossing 1.0 is the Lancet paper's framing, not the trial's registered one.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] This sentence is accurate and appropriately hedged. No finding.
     quote         : “Adjuvant melanoma trials have a history of recurrence benefits that took years to translate into survival benefits, and sometimes never did.”
     evidence says : The sentence is accurate as written.
     fix           : No change needed.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · CONTRADICTION] CheckMate 238 compares nivolumab to ipilimumab — it has no placebo arm. The draft acknowledges this immediately after ('CheckMate 238's arms are nivolumab and ipilimumab, so there is no placebo arm to report against'), but the sentence itself includes CheckMate 238 in a list of trials described as testing 'against placebo as a prespecified endpoint in its own population.' A reader quoting this sentence alone will take CheckMate 238 as a PD-1-vs-placebo trial that failed to show OS benefit, which is wrong on two counts: it is not a PD-1 inhibitor trial (nivolumab is PD-1, but the comparator is ipilimumab, a CTLA-4 inhibitor, not placebo), and the sentence's own framing ('against placebo') does not apply to it.
     quote         : “For adjuvant PD-1 inhibitors specifically — the comparison arm in this very trial — in the three trials whose registry records we hold — KEYNOTE-054, KEYNOTE-716 and CheckMate 238 — none has reported a statistically significant overall survival benefit against placebo as a prespecified endpoint in its own population”
     evidence says : KEYNOTE-054 and KEYNOTE-716 are the relevant PD-1-vs-placebo trials. CheckMate 238 is a PD-1 vs CTLA-4 trial with no placebo arm and should not appear in a list qualified by 'against placebo.' The draft corrects this in the next sentence but the sentence itself is wrong.
     fix           : Remove CheckMate 238 from the parenthetical list: 'in the two trials whose registry records we hold — KEYNOTE-054 and KEYNOTE-716 — neither has reported a statistically significant overall survival benefit against placebo as a prespecified endpoint in its own population.' Then retain the existing note about CheckMate 238 separately.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The sentence is grammatically ambiguous: 'it does' could be read as 'it does cost nothing' (contradicting the intended meaning) or 'it does cost something' (the intended meaning). The intended reading is that the added therapy does carry a cost in grade 3+ adverse events, but the sentence construction invites the opposite reading.
     quote         : “Reading only the first pair would tell you the added therapy costs nothing, and it does.”
     evidence says : The intended meaning is that the added therapy does carry a cost — grade 3 or worse treatment-related events were higher (25% vs 18%). The sentence should be rewritten to make this unambiguous.
     fix           : Replace 'Reading only the first pair would tell you the added therapy costs nothing, and it does' with 'Reading only the first pair would tell you the added therapy costs nothing in toxicity — but it does: grade 3 or worse treatment-related events were higher on the combination (25% vs 18%).'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft's own sources section confirms that the five-year KEYNOTE-942 data were published simultaneously in the Journal of Clinical Oncology and presented at ASCO 2026. The January 2026 topline release (also cited) reported the same HR=0.510 figure. The draft correctly attributes these to KEYNOTE-942. However, the phrase 'reduction in the hazard of recurrence or death' is the draft's own restatement of the companies' language 'reduction in the risk of recurrence or death.' The companies' language ('risk') is technically imprecise for a hazard ratio, but the draft's substitution of 'hazard' is the correct reading. This is not an error in the draft — it is the draft correcting the companies' language. No finding.
     quote         : “the 49% reduction in the hazard of recurrence or death, the 59% reduction in the hazard of distant metastasis or death, both from the five-year follow-up — describe a different trial: an open-label study of 157 patients”
